The integration test skips truthfully (ADR-013) with the verbatim EACCES —
the SAME skip behavior recorded across v5-5/v6-5/v8/v9. The render-path
enable-write ordering + non-zero write + clean-exit no-restore are
asserted IN-PROCESS by the unit tests (test_render_path_enable_write_
precedes_first_flush_and_update, test_clean_exit_no_backlight_restore_write),
which DO run (MockDisplay, no master required) and PASS. See
adversarial-demos-v10.log for the non-vacuity demo on the ordering test.

=== W13 adjudication ===

The W13 task asks: did the enable() addition break the sim path
(MockBacklight receives the write)? On this bench the sim path is not
reachable past acquire (environmental), so a LIVE sim smoke is not
executable. The IN-PROCESS unit tests (MockDisplay + RecordingBacklight
wrapping MockBacklight) exercise the SAME real_main render path that
the dry-run smoke would, WITHOUT the master-gated acquire, and assert:
  - calls[0] == "enable" (enable is the FIRST backlight call)
  - enable_idx < update_idx (enable precedes the loop-bottom update)
  - !write_log().is_empty() && *front() > 0 (MockBacklight received a
    non-zero enable write)
  - no set_brightness on clean SIGTERM exit (D3)
All PASS; the adversarial demo (demo 3) confirms the ordering test FAILS
if enable is moved after the first flush. The enable() addition did NOT
break the sim path's contract — it is exercised through the in-process
tests on every `cargo test`. A LIVE sim smoke on a bench with an
unmastered vkms card (or a VT session) is the merge session's
re-verification, same as every prior QA in this environment.
